    "result_text": "The entire suite consists of the single `AcceptanceTest` (the other files are base/helper classes), and it passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on the owner-create flow so that it reports the number of existing owners sharing the same first and last name (case-insensitively) at the moment before the new owner is created.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` (readOnly `int32`, min 0) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` gets the field and accessors.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field mapped to the `namesake_count` column, with getter/setter. This persists the value computed at create time so it's returned unchanged on later `GET`s.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 before saving (next to `customerCode` assignment), it computes and sets the count via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper that streams existing owners and matches names with `equalsIgnoreCase`. Because the count is taken over `findAllOwners()` before the new owner is saved, it correctly excludes the owner being created.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column. The tests run under the `hsqldb` profile with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column had to exist in `schema.sql`.\n\nMapStruct maps `namesakeCount` automatically by name (Owner \u2192 OwnerDto), so no mapper change was needed.\n\nIn the acceptance test, the first \"Ann Sur\u2026\" owner is stored with count 0, the second with count 1 (one existing namesake), and `GET` returns `namesakeCount = 1` as expected.",
